et vsauce into  your home  [Music]  you have a litter of kittens  or your  cat does let's say the cat has four  kittens and there's a 50-50 chance of  each kitten being a boy or a girl every  combination of kittens is 0.5 raised to  the power of 4 or .0625  but the most common combination isn't  the equal split of two boys two girls  you'd expect it's actually three of one  and one of the other when you map out  all the possible combinations of kittens  there are 16 combinations that all have  the same exact probability of happening  but getting a perfectly even two of each  happens in just six of the 16 combos or  37.5 percent a 3-1 split happens in 8 of  the 16 combinations  50 percent of the time you'll get 3 and  1 despite the 50-50 mass telling you  everything's got the same chance  simple math tells you one thing but  combinatorics using that math tells you  another  okay  let's get back to wallets do you have an  advantage in this game if your wallet  has less money in it you'll always win  more than that amount by taking the  other wallet that has more money in it  if your wallet has more money you'll  only lose what you've got  since the game is totally random and  neither player knows anything about the  other the probability of each player  winning and losing is the same  but when you win you'll get more money  than you lose when you don't win  so you've got a major advantage in this  game but when the other player loses  they're only losing what's in their  wallet and when they win they're winning  more than what's in their wallet they've  got a major advantage in this game  the question is how can two players  competing against each other both have  an advantage here's how they can't if  there are two outcomes x and y we can  assign probabilities to each and adding  those probabilities together has to add  up to 1. having an advantage would mean  a probability greater than 0.5 if x and  y are both greater than 0.5 you're going  to get more than one when